// Element-wise add (split) — same arithmetic as example 01, but the
// driving example for the smallest cross-worker decomposition: host
// loads the inputs, a compute worker performs the per-element add,
// host writes the output.
//
// What this example demonstrates:
//   - The algorithm/schedule split survives a real cross-worker edge:
//     the same prog.algo.nuc compiles under a single-worker schedule
//     (naive.sched.nuc — smoke test) and under a two-worker schedule
//     (split.sched.nuc — host + w0, with declared transfers).
//   - The first `transfer` directives in the example matrix: arrays
//     `a`, `b` cross from `host` to `w0`; array `c` crosses back.
//
// What this example does NOT demonstrate:
//   - Distributed placement of the add across multiple compute workers
//     (a single `w0` runs the whole loop here). That's a later example.
//   - Blocking, vectorising, async / buffered transfers, halo regions.
//     Each gets its own example so the differential test can isolate
//     what breaks.
//
// Numeric choice: i32. PRD §10.1 — integers are bit-deterministic by
// language definition; reorderable floating-point reductions are not.
// Consistent with example 01 and every later integer example
// (reduction, prefix sum, histogram, sort) where determinism actually
// bites. The element-wise add has no reduction, so f32 would have
// worked without controversy — i32 wins on consistency.
//
// Dataflow shape (deliberately identical to example 01)
// -----------------------------------------------------
// Three top-level statements plus a for-loop:
//
//   a <-- load_input();        // produced on host (by schedule)
//   b <-- load_input_b();      // produced on host (by schedule)
//   for i : 0 .. N {
//       c[i] <-- add(a[i], b[i]);   // consumed/produced on w0
//   }
//   save_output(c);            // consumed on host (by schedule)
//
// The algorithm itself has NO knowledge of `host` or `w0`. That is the
// point of the algorithm/schedule split (PRD §2). Which kernel runs
// where, and which data crosses workers, lives entirely in the
// schedule.

const N : usize = 256;     // 256 * 4 bytes per array = 1 KiB; matches example 01.

// Whole-program input/output arrays. Single-assignment per PRD §6.2.1.
data a : i32[N];
data b : i32[N];
data c : i32[N];


// Scalar add. Pure: reorderable, deduplicable, eliminable. Signature
// is (i32, i32) -> i32 — explicitly scalar so the contract pass
// (TASK-0012) matches it against `pub fn add(a: i32, b: i32) -> i32`
// without needing aggregate machinery.
kernel add : (i32, i32) -> i32 pure;

// I/O kernels. Effectful: ordering preserved, never duplicated.
// Aggregate-typed in Nuc; on the Rust side `Vec<i32>` (see kernels.rs
// and the README's "Why Vec<i32>" section) sidesteps TASK-0103.
kernel load_input  : ()       -> i32[N] effectful;
kernel load_input_b : ()      -> i32[N] effectful;
kernel save_output : (i32[N]) -> ()     effectful;


// Dataflow.
a <-- load_input();
b <-- load_input_b();

for i : 0 .. N {
    c[i] <-- add(a[i], b[i]);
}

save_output(c);
